    "lawCode": "Amd §413, Pers Prop L; amd §108, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Personal Property Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2460\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the personal property law and the banking law, in\nrelation to excluding finance, service or interest charges and other\nauthorized fees from the amount used to determine whether the\nspecified credit limit has been exceeded for the purpose of imposing\nan over limit charge in retail instalment credit agreements and other\ninstallment obligations\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nRequires that creditors and financial institutions exclude all\nfinance, interest and service charges and/or other authorized fees,\nsuch as late and annual fees, from the amount used to determine a\nbalance with the explicit purpose of imposing \"over the limit\"\ncharges.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nThe closing paragraph of Section 1, Subdivision 5 of Section 413 of\nthe Personal Property Law is designated Paragraph (c), and Paragraph\n(b) of such Subdivision is amended by Chapter 1 of the Laws of 1994.\nThis bill also amends Subparagraph (vii) of Paragraph (e) of\nSubdivision 5 of Section 108 of the Banking Law, as amended by Chapter\n1 of the Laws of 1994.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nCurrent banking laws permit creditors and financial institutions to\ncharge \"over-the-limit\" fees on account balances that exceed the\nmaximum credit allowed. However, finance charges, annual fees and/or\nlate fees are added to the balance, and are often directly responsible\nfor causing the monthly total to exceed the maximum credit limit. This\npractice, which can be repeated each succeeding payment period, pushes\nthe balance over the limit and then penalizes the account holder by\nassessing an over the limit charge.  Accordingly, this bill will\nrequire that creditors and financial institutions exclude finance,\ninterest and service charges and/or other authorized fees, such as\nlate and annual fees. As a result, this bill will ensure that balances\non purchases only are subject to over the limit fees, and help protect\nconsumers from incurring excessive, unreasonable fees.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law and shall apply to retail installment credit agreements\nunder section 413 of the personal property law and to installment\nobligations under section 108 of the banking law, that are issued,\nrenewed, amended or restated on or after the effective date of this\nact.\n",
